TWO THINGS LEARNED BY RUNNING IT RATHER THAN REASONING ABOUT IT:
  * A MATRIX WORKFLOW PUBLISHES ONE CONTEXT PER LEG. nightly.yml reports
    .../nightly/1 .. /4 and NO bare .../nightly, so the first cut reported a
    live workflow as having no verdict. Legs now group under their declaring
    workflow and are named individually -- the readable half of what INV-libib
    records ("all four legs return one identical log and cannot be addressed
    individually").
  * A COMMENT IS NOT A DECLARATION. The expected set is derived from
    .woodpecker/*.yml, and woodpecker.yml DOCUMENTS its cron siblings in prose
    ("full-suite.yml ... (push-to-dev + `event: cron` named ...)"), so a bare
    grep enrolled a workflow that declares no cron. Comments are stripped first.

Also fixed here, because it bit twice in one session: `grep -c . || echo 0`
prints grep's own 0 AND the fallback, making a two-line string that `[[ -ge ]]`
cannot parse -- the same defect this session fixed in smart-test's manifest
header. `|| true` keeps the count as the only output.

ci-debug-protocol.md CARRIED A FALSE CLAIM, corrected in the same change. It
named the nightly contexts `nightly/test-matrix` / `nightly/integration-tests`
and asserted "`ci-debug status` works for nightly runs too". Neither is true:
the contexts are the four matrix legs, and `status` is precisely the reader that
cannot see them.

